# The ROM's USB download console, driven the way esptool drives it.
#
# Written by hand rather than by esptool so the exchange is
# deterministic: every chunk carries the EMULATED MILLISECOND it
# arrives at (the `--usb-script` grammar's leading number is
# milliseconds, as `rom-reset-into-download.usb`'s `1200` is), and
# the reply is whatever the real mask ROM makes of it. Nothing here
# is a model of esptool — these are the bytes on the wire,
# SLIP-framed, with the 36-byte SYNC payload the protocol fixes
# (0x07 0x07 0x12 0x20 then 32 x 0x55).
#
# History: M7 committed this file with `20000` and `40000` believing
# them microseconds. Under the grammar they were 20 and 40 SECONDS,
# past the end of every run that ever attached the file, so the
# console's "silence" was a SYNC that had not been sent yet (M3 P1,
# docs/reports/2026-09-08-esp32c6-rom-console-trace.md). The bytes
# were right all along; only the stamps moved.
#
# Replayed as a gate by tests/rom_download_console.rs.

# The ROM has printed `waiting for download` well before this (the
# banner is done by 1.2 ms of emulated time).
20 c0 00 08 24 00 00 00 00 00 07 07 12 20 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 55 c0

# READ_REG on EFUSE_RD_MAC_SPI_SYS_0 (0x600b0844): the low four
# bytes of the MAC, which `--efuse-mac` decides and the eFuse
# block serves. A reply proves the console is executing commands,
# not just echoing a sync.
40 c0 00 0a 04 00 00 00 00 00 44 08 0b 60 c0
